# Run Local Flask Application

The goal is to start the Flask application locally using Docker Compose since the user encountered issues with GitHub. We will focus on the `flask-dev` service which includes hot-reloading and debug mode.

## Proposed Changes

We will update the [docker-compose.yml](file:///c:/projects/github/casatozima/docker-compose.yml) to include a local PostgreSQL service and update the Flask services to depend on it. This ensures a fully functional local environment.

### [MODIFY] [docker-compose.yml](file:///c:/projects/github/casaos-home-server/flask-app/docker-compose.yml)
- Add a `db` service using the `postgres:16.2` image.
- Update `DATABASE_URL` in Flask services to point to the new `db` container.
- Add `depends_on` to ensure the database starts before the Flask app.

### Docker Compose
- Run `docker compose up -d` to start both the database and the development container.

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- Check container status: `docker ps --filter name=flask-dev`
- Check logs: `docker logs flask-dev`

### Manual Verification
- Access the application at `http://localhost:5007` in the browser.
- Verify the index page loads correctly.
